Search {{ CLI_NAME }} patterns for "$ARGUMENTS".

1. Run `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern search -q "$ARGUMENTS"`.
2. If a pattern matches (score > 0):
   - Run `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern run <name> --var key=value ... -d` to preview.
   - Show the user what will be generated and where.
   - On approval, run without `-d` to write the files.
   - Note: a pattern may include subpatterns — all included files
     appear in the preview/output automatically.
3. If no pattern matches:
   - Generate the new feature as requested.
   - After generating, ask the user whether they'd like to capture it as a pattern.
   - If yes, run `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern gen -n <short-name> -i "$ARGUMENTS" -v "..." -o '...'`,
     fill in the template (prefer a `.ts` file whose default export is a function
     `(vars: Record<string, string>) => string` — the CLI calls it with the declared
     variables keyed by name and writes the returned string to `outputPath`; keep
     it a pure function of `vars`, e.g.
     `export default ({ name }: Record<string, string>) => \`# ${name}\`;`.
     Use `.njk` only when a `.ts` template is impractical),
     and run `{{ CLI_NAME }} pattern validate -n <name>`.